{"key": "g05", "title": "Particles of the Qur'an 5: Kallaa, Tanween and the Ending of Emphasis", "subtitle": "Four small pieces you see on every page", "trigger_at": "2026-12-01T13:00:00.000Z", "body": {"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "captionedImage", "content": [{"type": "image2", "__img": "cover-g05.png", "alt": "Particles of the Qur'an 5: Kallaa, Tanween and the Ending of Emphasis"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Particles of the Qur'an, part 5 of 5", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "We finish the particles with four small pieces you see on almost every page of the Qur'an."}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"textAlign": null, "level": 3}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "1. \"No! Never!\": كَلَّا (kallaa)"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The particle of stopping and scolding ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "harf ar-rad'", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": ", حَرْفُ الرَّدْعِ). It rejects what came before: \"No, it is not as you think!\""}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "In the Qur'an: كَلَّا سَوْفَ تَعْلَمُونَ \"No! You will soon know\" (102:3)."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Sometimes it means \"truly\", like حَقًّا \"truly, really\": كَلَّا إِنَّ الْإِنسَانَ لَيَطْغَىٰ \"Truly, man goes beyond limits\" (96:6)."}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"textAlign": null, "level": 3}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "2. The two kinds of ت"}]}, {"type": "bullet_list", "content": [{"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The round ta (ة):", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " at the end of a noun it usually marks the feminine, as in جَنَّة \"garden\". It carries a vowel, so it is called \"the moving ta\" ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "at-taa' al-mutaharrikah", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": ", التَّاءُ الْمُتَحَرِّكَةُ)."}]}]}, {"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The long ta (تْ):", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " at the end of a past verb it means \"she\". It carries a sukun, so it is called \"the silent ta\" ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "at-taa' as-saakinah", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": ", التَّاءُ السَّاكِنَةُ). فَلَمَّا سَمِعَتْ \"when she heard\" (12:31)."}]}]}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"textAlign": null, "level": 3}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "3. Tanween (nunation)"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Tanween ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "tanween", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": ", تَنْوِين) is an \"n\" sound after the last vowel of a noun. It is written by doubling the vowel mark, not with a letter ن: كِتَابٌ \"a book\" ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "kitaabun", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": "), كِتَابًا ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "kitaaban", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": "), كِتَابٍ ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "kitaabin", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": "). It usually shows that the noun is indefinite (\""}, {"type": "text", "text": "a", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " book\"). It is not emphasis."}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"textAlign": null, "level": 3}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "4. The ن of emphasis"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The ن of emphasis ("}, {"type": "text", "text": "noon at-tawkeed", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": ", نُونُ التَّوْكِيدِ) is added to the end of a verb to make it stronger: \"he "}, {"type": "text", "text": "will certainly", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": "…\". It comes in two forms:"}]}, {"type": "bullet_list", "content": [{"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Heavy, with shadda (نَّ):", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " لَيُسْجَنَنَّ \"he will surely be imprisoned\" (12:32)."}]}]}, {"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Light, with sukun (نْ):", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " وَلَيَكُونًا مِّنَ الصَّاغِرِينَ \"and he will surely be among the humbled\" (12:32). In the Qur'an the light ن is written like tanween. It appears only twice: here, and in لَنَسْفَعًا \"We will surely drag\" (96:15)."}]}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Both forms are in the same ayah, 12:32. The ن of emphasis goes only on verbs, mostly on commands, forbidding, questions, wishes, requests and oaths, as in these two examples, which follow an oath."}]}, {"type": "heading", "attrs": {"textAlign": null, "level": 3}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Check yourself"}]}, {"type": "ordered_list", "attrs": {"start": 1, "type": null, "order": 1}, "content": [{"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Which ت would you expect at the end of رَحْمَة \"mercy\", and why?"}]}]}, {"type": "list_item", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "In لَيُسْجَنَنَّ, which letters are the emphasis?"}]}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Answers:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " (1) The round ة: it is a noun. (2) The final نَّ."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "That completes the particles. "}, {"type": "text", "text": "Next:", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " the 25 most common nouns in the Qur'an."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "From my book", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": " Quran Grammar Summary "}, {"type": "text", "text": "(second edition), section 1.5.", "marks": [{"type": "em"}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "attrs": {"textAlign": null}, "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Download this post as a PDF", "marks": [{"type": "strong"}]}, {"type": "text", "text": ", with all the Arabic in Qur'anic script: "}, {"type": "text", "text": "Particles of the Qur'an 5: Kallaa, Tanween and the Ending of Emphasis (PDF)", "marks": [{"type": "link", "attrs": {"href": "https://www.seemabooks.com/downloads/substack-posts/g05-kallaa-tanween-and-the-ending-of-emphasi.pdf", "target": "_blank", "rel": "noopener noreferrer nofollow", "class": null}}]}]}]}}